[2026-10-24] Checked whether the all-zero ISO clash rates are an instrument failure.
             They are not, and the check is recorded because the zeros look suspicious:
               - the same counter fires on other arms in the SAME run: copy-the-parent
                 791/1452 examples (max frame rate 0.92), HARM 9-75, leaky Sigma* 22-47,
                 Control A at s=5.0 89. A dead counter could not produce those.
               - zero is not specific to ISO: leaky GS is also 0/1452 on every fold and
                 leaky LAP is 0/1/1, i.e. every under-dispersed arm gives zero.
               - clash rate increases monotonically with internal RMSF across arms.
               - decisive: in the REFERENCE MD itself (40 random systems) the minimum
                 ligand-heavy / pocket-heavy separation has median 2.55 A, p5 2.48 A and
                 never falls below 2.43 A. No system ever reaches 2.0 A, let alone 1.2 A.
             So 1.2 A is a severe-penetration threshold that real trajectories never
             cross, and zero is the correct and expected value for any ensemble that
             stays near the input pose.
             CONSEQUENCE, and a manuscript fix: the clash column is floor-saturated and
             is NOT a quality ranking. As first written, Table \ref{tab:clean} showed
             ISO 0.0% beside HARM 1.8% in a lower-is-better column, which a referee would
             read as the isotropic arm being physically better. The caption and the text
             now state that the threshold is about half a real contact distance, that the
             reference trajectories never reach it, that an under-dispersed ensemble
             scores zero regardless of quality, and that the rate is informative only
             where non-zero.
